<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- Lets Xcode attach a debugger. Debug only: the Release entitlements file
	     is empty, and scripts/lib/bundle-identity-checks.sh asserts that. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.get-task-allow</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- ovation#59, and Dan signed this off on 2026-09-06 as a Debug only
	     change.

	     WHY IT IS HERE. Under hardened runtime macOS refuses to load code into a
	     process unless it is signed with a matching Team ID. Ovation's signing
	     identity deliberately has none, so the app and the hosted test bundle
	     both report TeamIdentifier=not set, and macOS treats two unset teams as
	     different rather than the same. The hosted bundle therefore could not
	     load at all:

	       The bundle "OvationHostedTests" couldn't be loaded ...
	       mapping process and mapped file (non-platform) have different Team IDs

	     This is the same class of failure as ovation#30, which was solved by
	     REMOVING the thing that needed the exemption (the Debug dylib). That
	     route does not exist here: loading a bundle into the app is what a
	     hosted test IS.

	     WHAT IT COSTS, stated rather than discovered later. On the DEBUG build
	     only, macOS stops checking that loaded code is signed by the same
	     identity. That build already carries get-task-allow above, so any
	     process can already attach a debugger and read its memory; it is not a
	     security boundary. The Release build keeps hardened runtime, an empty
	     entitlements file, and now a check asserting it never gains this key.

	     The alternatives, and why not: turning hardened runtime off on Debug
	     disables protections nothing here needs disabled, and a paid Apple
	     Developer team would make the signatures match but costs money for a
	     problem an entitlement solves for nothing. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
